---
// source: 854c3338933e
import FigureTable from '../../components/FigureTable.astro';
import CaseLink from '../../components/CaseLink.astro';
import { benefitBySalary } from '../../content/examples';
import { euros, wholeEuros } from '../../content/format';
import { BENEFIT_2026 } from '../../engine/unemployment';
import { link, type Lang } from '../../i18n/languages';

interface Props {
  lang: Lang;
}
const { lang } = Astro.props;

const { minCap, maxCap } = BENEFIT_2026;
const [, mid, high] = benefitBySalary;
if (!mid || !high) throw new Error('Incomplete example');
type Row = (typeof benefitBySalary)[number];
const stretch = (b: Row, n: 0 | 1 | 2) => b.children[n] ?? { c1: 0, c2: 0 };
// One row per figure, one column per salary, so the table fits a phone.
const rows: readonly (readonly [string, (b: Row) => number])[] = [
  ['Benefit base (base reguladora)', (b) => b.base],
  ['First 180 days, no children', (b) => stretch(b, 0).c1],
  ['First 180 days, with one', (b) => stretch(b, 1).c1],
  ['First 180 days, with two or more', (b) => stretch(b, 2).c1],
  ['From day 181, no children', (b) => stretch(b, 0).c2],
];
---

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="p-cuanto">70&nbsp;% and then 60&nbsp;%</h3>
  <p>
    Unemployment benefit (paro), the contributory unemployment benefit (prestación contributiva por
    desempleo), is calculated on your benefit base (base reguladora): the average of the
    unemployment contributions paid in the last 180 days, without overtime (art. 270.1 LGSS). You
    receive 70&nbsp;% of that base for the first 180 days and 60&nbsp;% from day 181 (art. 270.2
    LGSS).
  </p>
  <p>
    Each month has a minimum and a maximum depending on your dependent children (art. 270.3 LGSS).
    In 2026, with the IPREM (the public income reference index) at{' '}
    {wholeEuros(BENEFIT_2026.monthlyIprem)}, they are these:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      With no dependent children, between {wholeEuros(minCap[0])} and {wholeEuros(maxCap[0])} a
      month.
    </li>
    <li>
      With one, between {wholeEuros(minCap[1])} and {wholeEuros(maxCap[1])}.
    </li>
    <li>
      With two or more, between {wholeEuros(minCap[1])} and {wholeEuros(maxCap[2])}.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>270</span> LGSS
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="p-tabla">
    How much unemployment benefit you receive on a payslip of 1.300, 1.500 or 1.800&nbsp;€
  </h3>
  <FigureTable id="p-tabla-titulo">
    <Fragment slot="caption">
      Gross unemployment benefit a month with pay in 14 equal payments and full-time hours, in 2026,
      rounded to the euro as in the calculator.
    </Fragment>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Gross pay a month</th>
        {benefitBySalary.map(({ salary }) => (
          <th scope="col">{wholeEuros(salary)}</th>
        ))}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {rows.map(([label, value]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{label}</th>
          {benefitBySalary.map((b) => (
            <td>{wholeEuros(value(b))}</td>
          ))}
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </FigureTable>
  <p>
    The base comes from the annual pay, including the extra payments, divided by 12: with
    1.500&nbsp;€ in 14 payments, 21.000&nbsp;€ / 12 = {euros(mid.base)}, and 70&nbsp;% of it is
    exactly the maximum with no dependent children. With 1.800&nbsp;€ the base is {euros(high.base)}{' '}
    and 70&nbsp;% of it goes above the maximums with no children and with one, so in those cases you
    receive the maximum.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are gross figures. From them, 4,85&nbsp;% of the base is deducted for the Seguridad
    Social, plus whatever IRPF (income tax) applies. With part-time hours the figures are lower, and
    the calculator does not give them.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="p-requisitos">Who can receive it</h3>
  <p>
    The contract has to end in a legal situation of unemployment: for example, a dismissal or the
    end of a fixed-term contract that you did not end yourself (art. 267 LGSS). You also need at
    least 360 days of contributions in the last 6 years, to register as a jobseeker and to sign the
    activity agreement (acuerdo de actividad), among other requirements (art. 266 LGSS).
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      How long it lasts depending on your contributions:{' '}
      <CaseLink lang={lang} id="benefit_duration" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you are the one leaving: <CaseLink lang={lang} id="benefit_resignation" />
    </li>
    <li>
      If you are dismissed for misconduct: <CaseLink lang={lang} id="benefit_disciplinary" />.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Without registering as a jobseeker there is no unemployment benefit: you register with your
    region’s employment service and apply to SEPE within the 15 days after the contract ends, or
    after the end of the untaken holiday paid in your final settlement (arts. 266 and 268 LGSS).
    SEPE states that these are business days.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Arts. <span>266 · 267 · 268</span> LGSS
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="p-erte">If you are in an ERTE</h3>
  <p>
    An ERTE (temporary lay-off scheme) can also give entitlement to unemployment benefit while it
    lasts. How much you receive and whether it uses up your future unemployment benefit depend on
    the type of ERTE:{' '}
    <a href={link(lang, 'paro/erte/')}>calculate your unemployment benefit during an ERTE</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Arts. <span>262 · 267</span> LGSS, DA <span>41.ª · 46.ª</span>
  </p>
</section>
